\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 INDIA, Jan. 14: From before dawn until after dusk, meticulously prepared kites take to the Ahmedabad sky for Uttarayan, India\u8217 s International Kite Festival. During the festival\u8217 s \u8220 kite fights,\u8221  competitors attempt to cut down each other\u8217 s kites, a feat met with the triumphant cry of \u8220 kai po che!\u8221  or \u8220 I have cut!\u8221  But the strings that maneuver these spectacles, called manja, have a sinister past. Coated with metal or powdered glass to facilitate the slashing of other kites, manja have become known as the \u8220 strings of death\u8221  for entangling and fatally slashing the necks of those in its path. Ahead of the festival, the Ahmedabad police seize thousands of reels of the banned string from sellers in markets around the city.}
